@page "/docs"
@layout Layout.DocsLayout

<PageTitle>Introduction · blux-ui</PageTitle>
<div class="pg-prose">
    <p class="pg-eyebrow">Getting started</p>
    <h1>Introduction</h1>
    <p class="pg-lead">blux-ui is an open-source component library for Blazor, styled with Tailwind CSS v4 and designed around Blazor's render modes.</p>

    <h2>Why another component library</h2>
    <p>
        A Blazor component can run on the server over SignalR, in the browser on WebAssembly, switch between both in Auto mode,
        or render once as static HTML. Most libraries assume one of these. In Server mode, a menu that opens through a .NET
        handler waits for a network round trip on every click and every arrow key.
    </p>
    <p>
        blux-ui splits the work: what only changes the look (opening, closing, focus, keyboard navigation, positioning) runs in
        the browser through small custom elements, and .NET is told only about the state you bind. The same components then work
        in every render mode, including static pages.
    </p>

    <h2>What you get</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>20 components</strong>: buttons, forms with validation, overlays, tabs, accordion, table, toasts.</li>
        <li><strong>Accessibility</strong> following the WAI-ARIA authoring practices, checked with axe in light and dark mode.</li>
        <li><strong>Theming</strong> with CSS variables, a global theme in C#, and per-instance slot overrides.</li>
        <li><strong>Tailwind optional</strong>: use the precompiled stylesheet, or compile everything with your own Tailwind build.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Next steps</h2>
    <p><a href="/docs/installation">Install the package</a>, then browse the <a href="/components/button">components</a>.</p>
</div>
